  "slug": "ancient-inference-e6e8",
  "summary": "Four preprints introduce scalable inference tools for ancient DNA relatedness, hominin mtDNA mixtures, and deep eukaryote phylogeny.",
  "synthesis": "The four preprints share a methodological focus: computational frameworks that extract evolutionary relationships from historical or deep-time genomic data. Fastpmr provides a fast, memory-efficient pairwise mismatch-rate method for biological relatedness inference across the Allen Ancient DNA Resource. TrailMix estimates mixture proportions and phylogenetic placement of hominin mtDNA lineages in ancient samples, using a pangenomic reference graph. Two further studies address deep phylogeny and gene family evolution. One uses 219 pre-LECA ancient gene duplication families and phylogenetic reconciliation to test candidate root positions of the eukaryote tree. H2O combines Ks distributions, tree-based duplication calls and paralogy-aware filtering to improve detection of large-scale gene family expansions. What is new is the application of these inferences to large archives and the explicit handling of mixture, paralogy and duplication evidence.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
